<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:system="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime"
                    xmlns:windows="clr-namespace:System.Windows;assembly=PresentationFramework">

    <!--
        The scale this product is built on - how big a gap is, how big a letter is, what shape a
        mark is. Not one colour, not one column width, and not one style.

        ADR-23. Everything else refers to these by name, and a guard in the architecture
        tests fails the build on a value written into a view. The reason is not tidiness:
        XAML is prose, a margin of seven instead of eight breaks nothing, and that is exactly
        how an interface drifts into looking like two products.

        THE THEME WAS ONE FILE UNTIL 2026-08-11, AND THE PROMISE IT CARRIED HAS TO BE
        RESTATED RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED. ADR-23 said that reading the theme from top to
        bottom is the same thing as knowing what the product looks like. That is now several
        readings rather than one, and each seam is the honest one - there was no style above the
        first line and no value below it, and the second fell between the list and everything
        else - but they are seams, and the guard that used to name one file now names the set.

        Why it was split, and the third time is 2026-08-17. The markup ratchet stood at 799 lines
        and this file had reached 849, so every further change to the window was refused by the
        build until somebody found a seam. Backlog 156 carries that plan and 157 carries why a
        ceiling set on a file under active work fires almost at once, which is the ceiling working
        rather than failing. The second split came the same evening, when the configurable columns
        of S6d2 needed cell templates in a dictionary and the styles half stood exactly on its new
        ceiling of 530. Backlog 163 had measured that seam in advance.

        THE THIRD SPLIT IS THE VALUES THEMSELVES, owner's decision, and it is what backlog 189
        was waiting on. This file stood exactly on the ceiling of 436, which blocked two things at
        once: a new column needs a width here and a new mark needs a brush here. The two largest
        blocks left, measured before the cut rather than guessed - 162 lines of brushes and 85 of
        column widths - and each answers a question somebody arrives with on its own. Colours.xaml
        answers "what colour" and Columns.xaml answers "how wide is that column".

        THE VALUE FILES ARE MERGED BEFORE THE STYLES FILES AND THAT IS LOAD BEARING. The styles
        reach these keys through StaticResource, which is resolved WHILE THE FILE IS BEING READ -
        so the order in App.xaml is WPF UI's dictionaries, then the value files, then Controls,
        then List, then Cells. None of them loads on its own.

        The base look comes from WPF UI in dark mode (ADR-24), so what lives here is only
        what is ours: the spacing scale, the type sizes, and the shapes a mark is drawn in.
    -->

    <!--
        A SPACING SCALE OF FOUR STEPS STOOD HERE UNTIL 2026-09-02 AND NOTHING HAD EVER USED IT.
        SpaceTight, SpaceNormal, SpaceWide and SpaceSection - zero references in markup, zero in
        code. Its own comment said "the guard makes the alternative fail the build", and there was
        no such guard: every margin in this window is a named Thickness instead, written one at a
        time. That sentence is the exact shape rule 3 warns about - a "because" nothing checks,
        read by later sessions as a fact. Backlog 294 keeps the intent, which was a good one.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="SizeNone">0</system:Double>

    <Thickness x:Key="PadCell">8,4,8,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        The list cell, and it is deliberately not PadCell.

        Measured on screen 2026-08-02: WPF UI's cell template honours TemplateBinding Padding
        and the built-in one did not, so the same 8,4,8,4 that was invisible before the
        migration became 6.6 logical pixels of row height after it - 29 rows on screen fell to
        22. The owner chose to keep this list's density, so the vertical half goes to nothing
        and row height is decided by the line height alone.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadListCell">8,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PadPanel">16</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBetweenControls">0,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginNone">0</Thickness>

    <!-- The mark a meaningful cell wears, and the gap between it and its word. -->
    <system:Double x:Key="ShapeMark">8</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="ShapeOutline">1.5</system:Double>

    <!--
        THE OUTLINE A MARK WEARS WHEN NOBODY COULD READ THE THING IT DESCRIBES, and it exists
        because until 2026-08-10 that case was drawn as an ORDINARY HOLLOW CIRCLE in two columns,
        separated from a real answer by nothing but two points of grey per channel.

        Status said "stopped" with a hollow ring in #A0A0A0 and "could not read" with a hollow ring
        in #9E9E9E. Start said "disabled" and "could not read" the same way. CellFaces.cs opens by
        promising that shape and word are two channels and neither is optional - and for the one
        case where the window knows the least, the shape channel was carrying nothing.

        A broken ring for broken knowledge. Values are multiples of the stroke thickness, so 2,2 at
        1.5 gives roughly four dashes around an eight pixel circle - a difference visible without
        comparing two marks side by side, which is the only way a colour difference that small can
        be seen at all.
    -->
    <DoubleCollection x:Key="ShapeUnreadableOutline">2,2</DoubleCollection>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAfterShape">0,0,8,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE GAP BETWEEN A NUMBER AND THE WORDS IT COUNTS, AND IT WAS MISSING UNTIL 2026-09-01.
        The overview - the first screen anybody sees - read "113services running", "1set to start
        automatically and did not come up", "0orphans". Every line of it.

        WHY IT WAS MISSING IS WORTH MORE THAN THE VALUE. The label carried MarginBetweenControls,
        which is 0,0,8,0 - a gap on its RIGHT, where nothing stands - so it pushed away from what
        came after it and left nothing between itself and the number in front. A margin named for
        what it separates rather than for which side it is on reads as though it does both.

        FOUND BY LOOKING AT THE SCREEN. Backlog 192's package asked for the first run screen to be
        seen by an eye rather than counted by automation, and this is what the eye was for: every
        guard in this project passed over it, because a missing space is not a missing element.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAfterNumber">0,0,8,0</Thickness>

    <!-- A gravestone, cut 2026-09-02 from twenty-three lines of history attached to no value.
         The fact worth keeping: **WPF has no TextBlock.MaxLines**, so a line cap has to be a
         height AND an explicit line box. Backlog 192 is open again. -->

    <!--
        THE NARROWEST A CARD ON THE FIRST SCREEN MAY GET BEFORE THE ROW WRAPS - backlog 277, read
        off the screen that works. At the default the cards come out about 340 and every label sits
        on one or two lines. At 1000 by 800 they were 275 device pixels with labels five lines deep.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthOverviewCardLeast">300</system:Double>

    <!--
        HOW SMALL THIS WINDOW MAY BE MADE - backlog 278, both numbers measured. At 1000 by 800 the
        plan panel cut its own PRIMARY ACTION in half: its rows are Auto, Auto, star, Auto, and a
        Grid whose Auto rows want more than there is clips from the BOTTOM. 640 is where the seven
        actions stop being cut. The rejected alternative is in `docs/11` part 3.10.

        THE HEIGHT WAS 600 FOR ONE BUILD AND THE PHOTOGRAPH RAISED IT. The arithmetic forgot the
        block the steps now sit in, which spends 30 on its own margin and padding - so the button
        was there and the body was 53 points of nothing with a stray rule in it. Read off the
        picture: heading plus one step plus that chrome is 91, so 600 was 38 short.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightWindowLeast">640</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthWindowLeast">640</system:Double>

    <!--
        The mark for an entry running against its start type - backlog 165. A triangle rather than a
        dot, and why that matters is argued where the style is, in Cells.xaml. Eight by eight to
        match ShapeMark, scaled to fit, so all three marks occupy the same square.
    -->
    <Geometry x:Key="ShapeAgainst">M 4,0 L 8,8 L 0,8 Z</Geometry>

    <!--
        One pixel, and the outline is an OVERLAY rather than the row's own border. A border on
        the row takes layout space, and two pixels per row over 810 rows is about a tenth of
        the list - a price this window has twice refused to pay for looks, and refuses again
        for a ring that is only on one row at a time.

        IT STAYED HERE WHILE THE RING'S COLOUR WENT TO Colours.xaml ON 2026-08-17, and the split
        dropped it for one build. A thickness is a number and belongs with the scale - but it sat
        directly under the brush it goes with, so cutting the colours out by eye took it along.
        Three guards went red at once and all three said the same thing: a name referenced by a
        style and declared by nobody. That is the reference guard doing the job the emptiness one
        cannot, and it is why FocusRing and FocusRingThickness now sit in different files with a
        sentence in each saying so.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="FocusRingThickness">1</Thickness>

    <!--
        Fixed, because Auto here made the search box resize on every keystroke - the count shares
        its row and took pixels from the starred column as its sentence changed width. Backlog
        146, full reasoning in CHANGELOG-DEV 2026-08-10.
    -->
    <GridLength x:Key="WidthCountLine">210</GridLength>

    <!--
        HOW WIDE A TOOLTIP MAY GET BEFORE IT STOPS BEING READABLE - backlog 167.

        The three list columns are what asked for a number here. The longest required-privileges
        list on this machine holds 28 names, which is about 700 characters, and a tooltip does not
        wrap on its own - so it came out as one line across half the screen, unreadable exactly
        where it was supposed to help.

        Narrower than the empty-state sentence below on purpose. That one is prose somebody reads
        once and typography guidance puts a line of prose at about seventy characters. A tooltip is
        a list being scanned, sitting over the thing it describes, so it is better tall than wide.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthToolTip">420</system:Double>

    <!--
        How wide a sentence in the middle of an empty list may get before it stops being one.
        Around seventy characters at TextSizeHeading, which is the span typography guidance puts
        a line of prose at - a sentence stretched across 1650 pixels is read twice or not at all.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthEmptyState">520</system:Double>

    <!--
        HOW WIDE THE MACHINE OVERVIEW IS ALLOWED TO GET - `G`. Wider than the empty state, because
        every line here is a sentence rather than a phrase, and narrower than the window, because a
        line of prose running the full width of a maximised screen is one the eye loses its place in.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthOverview">720</system:Double>

    <!--
        The column the overview's numbers are right aligned in. Fixed rather than Auto, so that a
        machine answering 1 and a machine answering 1204 put their labels in the same place - which
        is complaint 3 of the eleven in `docs/11` arriving somewhere that is not a table.
    -->
    <GridLength x:Key="ColumnOverviewNumber">64</GridLength>

    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAboveStatus">0,8,0,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBelowSearch">0,0,0,8</Thickness>

    <!--
        Between one filter chip and the next, and it is the only new appearance value the whole
        clickable-filter slice needed.

        Right and bottom rather than all four sides, because the chips wrap: a gap on the leading
        edge would push the first chip of every line away from the search box above it, and the
        eye reads that as an indent rather than as spacing. The bottom half is what keeps two
        wrapped lines apart.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginChip">0,0,8,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        WHAT MAKES A CHIP LOOK LIKE A CHIP AND NOT LIKE A BUTTON - 2026-09-01, and the case for it
        is a measurement rather than taste. Read off the automation tree with the window at
        1650x1050: a filter chip was 47 pixels tall and an action button beside it was 48. Two
        controls that do completely different things - one writes a token into the query, the other
        opens a plan that can stop a service - were drawn as the same rounded rectangle at the same
        height with the same text size. `docs/11` complaint 14 is exactly this fault one control
        over, and its rule is the one being followed here: when the look carries the wrong MEANING,
        the repair is a whole visual family rather than a different shade of the old one.

        THREE SIGNALS RATHER THAN ONE, because any single one of them is arguable and three
        together are not: the corner becomes a pill, the height drops by about a fifth, and the
        text drops to the body size. A person does not have to notice any of them to stop confusing
        the two rows.

        AND IT PAYS FOR ITSELF IN THE ONE CURRENCY THIS WINDOW IS SHORT OF. Measured the same run:
        517 of 1050 pixels stand above the first row of data, and the four rows of chips are 206 of
        those. This takes about 36 back, which is a row of services - small, and in the right
        direction, which is more than the previous three attempts at this screen managed.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadChip">12,3,12,3</Thickness>

    <!--
        Inside the one button this product draws itself. It WAS 14,6 so the box would land on the
        library button's 48 device pixels, and the owner asked on 2026-09-01 for the elevate button
        to be smaller. Matching the neighbours' box was buying nothing here: a primary action is
        told apart by its fill, and this one never stands beside them anyway.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadButton">10,4,10,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE SCROLLBAR: ONE TARGET, TWO PAINTS, A RADIUS AND A FLOOR. The bar is what the pointer
        hits and the thumb is what gets drawn inside it, and keeping those two apart is the whole
        design - the argument, the measurements and the two remarks that produced them are in
        `docs/11` section 3.11, moved there when this file hit the markup ceiling.

        The last two are the FLOOR under the length and they are not read by anything here: Track
        looks the key up itself and takes half of it, so 72 gives 36 and ButtonGuards ties the pair.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthScrollBar">12</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthScrollThumb">6</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthScrollThumbAwake">10</system:Double>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerScrollThumb">2</CornerRadius>
    <system:Double x:Key="LengthScrollThumbLeast">36</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="{x:Static windows:SystemParameters.VerticalScrollBarButtonHeightKey}">72</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="{x:Static windows:SystemParameters.HorizontalScrollBarButtonWidthKey}">72</system:Double>

    <!--
        Larger than half the tallest chip on purpose. WPF clamps a corner radius to the box, so an
        over-large value is "a pill whatever the height turns out to be" rather than a number that
        has to be re-derived every time the padding or the type size moves.
    -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerChip">14</CornerRadius>

    <!--
        Grouping, in the filter row and in the column picker. The gap between facets is wider than
        the gap between chips on purpose - that difference is the only thing on screen saying where
        one question ends. The picker's ceiling is what makes it scroll instead of running off the
        bottom of a short screen and losing its last items silently.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginGroup">0,0,20,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE LINE UNDER THE SCOPE POSITION SOMEBODY IS ON, 2026-08-19. Three rather than one, because
        one pixel at 150% scaling is a hairline that reads as an artefact rather than as a mark -
        and this is the only thing on screen saying which of the two lists is showing.

        A LINE RATHER THAN A FILL, and that was the owner's correction on the day it was built. The
        first version borrowed the filter chip's appearance, which has an argued contrast pair and
        was the least invention - and it was wrong for a reason no guard could see: a position that
        looks exactly like a chip reads as another filter, and scope is not a filter. Fluent marks
        the selected item of a top navigation with an indicator line for the same reason.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightScopeUnderline">3</system:Double>

    <!--
        THE GAP ABOVE A SECTION HEADING, 2026-08-19, and it exists because MarginGroup has NO
        VERTICAL COMPONENT AT ALL - it is 0,0,20,0, a right margin. Every heading in the plan
        panel used it and every one of them therefore sat flush against the sentence above,
        which is what the owner saw as one wall of text with no hierarchy.

        Bottom is small on purpose: a heading belongs to what is under it, so the space above it
        has to be larger than the space below or the eye groups it with the wrong block.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginSection">0,18,0,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        Inside the plan panel, between its edge and its text. Without it the first character sits
        on the line separating the panel from the list, which is what made the two read as one
        surface however the fill was chosen.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingPanel">14,12,14,12</Thickness>

    <!-- The panel's one edge, on the side the list is. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderPanelEdge">1,0,0,0</Thickness>

    <!-- The rule above the panel's footer, so the button reads as part of the panel. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderFooterRule">0,1,0,0</Thickness>

    <!-- Between that rule and the button under it. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingFooter">0,10,0,0</Thickness>
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightMenu">420</system:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginGroupHeading">12,8,12,2</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE MACHINE OVERVIEW AS THREE CARDS RATHER THAN SIX LINES, 2026-09-01. Why the composition
        changed instead of the colours is at the head of OverviewView.xaml, beside the screen it is
        about - these are the steps that drawing needs and the three that are not reused say why.
    -->
    <!--
        EIGHT DOWN EACH SIDE RATHER THAN SIXTEEN, FROM 2026-09-01 EVENING, and the other eight
        moved onto the buttons inside. The card looks identical - a line still starts sixteen from
        its edge - and what changes is that the hover surface of a question now has air around the
        words instead of ending on the first letter. Photographed: the pointer lit a panel whose
        left boundary ran through the "1".
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadTile">8,14,8,16</Thickness>

    <!--
        BOXES WITH ALL FOUR EDGES, which is why neither is BorderPanelEdge - that one is 1,0,0,0,
        a seam rather than a thing. BorderAllSides is shared by the card and the plan sheet on
        purpose: one point is one point, and a second name for the same number is a second place to
        change it. Four for a card because WPF UI rounds its surfaces at four, eight for the sheet
        because it floats over a dimmed window rather than sitting in a row. The sheet's margin is
        also its height limit - centred inside it, it cannot outgrow the window. `docs/11` 3.12.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="BorderAllSides">1</Thickness>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerTile">4</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerPlanSheet">8</CornerRadius>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginPlanSheet">40</Thickness>

    <!--
        A FLOOR UNDER THE COPY BUTTON RATHER THAN LETTING IT FIT ITS WORD. That word becomes
        "Copied" for two seconds, and a button growing by two characters shoves the command beside
        it sideways - backlog 146 in a second place, where the search field moved the window.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthCopyButton">78</system:Double>

    <!--
        Between one card and the next. On the RIGHT ONLY, which leaves twelve points hanging off the
        last card of the row - accepted rather than answered with a negative margin on the row, which
        would be a value nothing on screen explains and a second thing to get wrong.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTile">0,0,12,0</Thickness>

    <!-- Between a card's number and its words, and above each block that follows the cards. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTileLabel">0,2,0,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTileParts">0,14,0,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        The column a qualification's own number is right aligned in. Narrower than
        ColumnOverviewNumber because these digits are set at 14 rather than 32, and a column sized
        for the headline strands a single digit far from the words it counts.
    -->
    <GridLength x:Key="ColumnQualificationNumber">30</GridLength>

    <!--
        HOW WIDE THE ROW OF CARDS MAY GET. Wider than WidthOverview, which caps PROSE and still does
        - the title, the hint and the footnote keep that cap inside this row, because a sentence and
        a row of cards do not want the same measure. Three cards here are about 340 each.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthOverviewFindings">1080</system:Double>
</ResourceDictionary>
